.Dd July 10, 2026
.Dt XTC_TAIL 3
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm xtc_tail_enable ,
.Nm xtc_tail_disable ,
.Nm xtc_tail_reset ,
.Nm xtc_tail_read ,
.Nm xtc_tail_dump ,
.Nm xtc_tail_count ,
.Nm xtc_tail_dropped
.Nd runtime microscope (individual scheduler-event recording)
.Sh LIBRARY
.Lb libxtc
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In xtc_tail.h
.Ft unsigned
.Fn xtc_tail_enable "unsigned source_mask"
.Ft void
.Fn xtc_tail_disable "void"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_tail_reset "void"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_tail_read "xtc_tail_fn cb" "void *user"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_tail_dump "int fd"
.Ft size_t
.Fn xtc_tail_count "void"
.Ft uint64_t
.Fn xtc_tail_dropped "void"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm xtc_tail
is a runtime microscope: it records every INDIVIDUAL runtime event,
tied to a precise instant, into a bounded ring -- so a degraded window
can be diffed against a normal one after the fact.  It is the
event-level complement to
.Xr xtc_stats 3
(aggregate counters) and
.Xr xtc_trace 3
(the causal message trace).  Inspired by dial9, "a microscope for
Tokio".
.Pp
Recording is OFF by default and costs a single predictable branch when
disabled, so it is safe to compile in for production and turn on only
while diagnosing.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_tail_enable
turns on the named event sources -- a bitwise OR of
.Dv XTC_TAIL_SCHED
(proc spawn / exit / wake / run),
.Dv XTC_TAIL_MSG
(message send / receive / mailbox depth high-water),
.Dv XTC_TAIL_IO ,
and
.Dv XTC_TAIL_OS
(the latter two reserved for the staged build-out).  The mask REPLACES
the current one and the previous mask is returned.
.Fn xtc_tail_disable
turns off all recording.
.Fn xtc_tail_reset
drops all buffered records.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_tail_read
visits every buffered record, oldest first, via
.Fa cb
(return nonzero to stop early); the snapshot is stable.
.Fn xtc_tail_count
returns how many records are buffered.
.Fn xtc_tail_dump
writes the buffer to
.Fa fd
as a versioned, compact, PORTABLE binary trace: an
.Vt xtc_tail_hdr_t
header
.Pq magic, version, endianness flag, count, base timestamp
written little-endian, followed by one varint/delta-encoded frame per
event (kind, source, LEB128 timestamp delta, pid, and detail).  The
delta-encoded timestamps and varint fields keep it to a handful of
bytes per event -- and being explicit little-endian with no struct
padding, a trace dumps and decodes identically across architectures --
for a separate offline tool to
render.
.Pp
Each
.Vt xtc_tail_rec_t
carries a monotonic timestamp, the source and kind, the proc pid, and a
kind-specific detail (an EXIT reason, or the wake-to-run latency in
nanoseconds for a RUN event -- the signal that exposes lost or late
wakeups).
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn xtc_tail_enable
returns the previously enabled source mask.
.Fn xtc_tail_reset ,
.Fn xtc_tail_read ,
and
.Fn xtc_tail_dump
return
.Dv XTC_OK
or a negative
.Dv XTC_E_*
code.
.Fn xtc_tail_count
returns the buffered record count.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_tail_dropped
returns how many records have been OVERWRITTEN because the ring wrapped
(total emitted minus what is still buffered).
.Pp
Check it before drawing any conclusion from the ABSENCE of an event.  With a
non-zero dropped count, "this pid has no events" and "this loop never polled"
are unfalsifiable -- the events may simply have been evicted.  A consumer
reported two verdicts that were exactly this artifact: 23 of 33 loops appeared
to have stopped polling when their events had merely been overwritten by a
higher-frequency event.  Conclusions drawn from events that are PRESENT remain
valid regardless.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_tail_disable
returns no value.
.Sh NOTES
Phase 1 implements the SCHED source (proc spawn / exit / park / run with
park-to-run latency) and the MSG source (send / receive / mailbox-depth
high-water), the in-process read, and the compact portable binary dump.
The IO and OS sources, an on-disk spill with rotation, and the offline
viewer are staged follow-ons; the record layout and the source mask are
stable so they extend the format without a break.
.Pp
Which parks are recorded, precisely, because the coverage is not uniform:
a
.Dv XTC_TAIL_PARK
/
.Dv XTC_TAIL_RUN
pair is emitted for a mailbox
.Fn xtc_recv
park, for the native async-file-I/O park inside
.Fn xtc_aio_pread ,
.Fn xtc_aio_pwrite ,
.Fn xtc_aio_fsync
and
.Fn xtc_aio_fdatasync ,
and for an
.Fn xtc_proc_wait_fd
readiness park -- which is the one most workloads spend their off-CPU time
in, since every
.Xr xtc_net 3
accept/read/write,
.Fn xtc_blocking_run
completion,
.Xr xtc_osproc 3
reap and
.Fn xtc_accel_wait_fence
parks through it.
For a
.Fn xtc_proc_wait_fd
park the
.Dv XTC_TAIL_PARK
.Fa detail
carries the file descriptor, so the event joins against the
.Li park_fd
column of
.Nm xtc-procs ;
for the other two sources it carries the aio opcode or zero.  In all cases
the
.Dv XTC_TAIL_RUN
.Fa detail
is the park-to-run latency in nanoseconds.
.Pp
Parks that are still
.Em not
recorded:
.Fn xtc_proc_sleep
(a pure timer park -- its wake instant is already known from the deadline)
and the internal condition-variable waits inside
.Xr xtc_sync 3 .
.Pp
The intended use of the pair is diagnostic: a
.Dv XTC_TAIL_PARK
with no matching
.Dv XTC_TAIL_RUN
for the same pid is a fiber that went to sleep and was never resumed --
a lost wakeup -- while a matching RUN whose
.Fa detail
(the park-to-run latency in nanoseconds) is large is a late one.
.Pp
.Dv XTC_TAIL_LOOP_POLL
is emitted by the SCHED source after each completed poll of a loop's own
I/O ring, even when the poll dispatched nothing
.Pq Fa detail Ns " = the number of events dispatched" .
Its
.Fa pid
carries only
.Fa loop_id ;
a loop is not a proc.  Its purpose is liveness: when a
.Dv XTC_TAIL_PARK
has no matching
.Dv XTC_TAIL_RUN ,
a continuing stream of
.Dv XTC_TAIL_LOOP_POLL
for that
.Fa loop_id
proves the loop kept turning and that one fiber was skipped, whereas the
absence of any proves the loop itself stopped.  Those have different causes
and different fixes, and without this event the only way to distinguish them
is to infer from the absence of other events -- which cannot tell a dead loop
apart from the end of the recording window.
.Pp
.Dv XTC_TAIL_WAKE
is emitted from the completion dispatch (the waker side).
Its
.Fa pid
carries only the dispatching
.Fa loop_id
-- dispatch has a task, not a proc -- and its
.Fa detail
is the task pointer as an integer, which is the join key against the
.Li task
column of
.Nm xtc-procs
and against
.Dv XTC_TAIL_PARK_TASK .
.Pp
.Em Note :
an earlier version of this page said to join
.Dv XTC_TAIL_WAKE
against the
.Fa detail
of the aio
.Dv XTC_TAIL_PARK ,
claiming it carried a task pointer.
It does not -- an aio PARK's
.Fa detail
is the OPCODE.
The two key spaces do not intersect, and that instruction
sent a consumer chasing a join that could not be performed.
.Dv XTC_TAIL_PARK_TASK
exists because of it.
.Pp
.Dv XTC_TAIL_PARK_TASK
is emitted immediately before the
.Dv XTC_TAIL_PARK
it describes, from the same fiber, and carries the PARKING proc's
.Fa pid
with that fiber's task pointer in
.Fa detail .
It exists solely to make
.Dv XTC_TAIL_WAKE
joinable: PARK carries the pid and, for an aio park, the opcode a consumer
needs to tell an fdatasync park from a read park, so overwriting that opcode
with the task pointer would trade one key for the other.
Emitting both gives
both.
All three park sites emit it -- aio,
.Fn xtc_proc_wait_fd
readiness, and mailbox recv.
.Pp
.Dv XTC_TAIL_REAP
records that a completion-queue entry was consumed by a reaping loop, and what
tag it resolved to.
Its
.Fa pid
carries only the reaping
.Fa loop_id ;
its
.Fa detail
is the same task pointer the other two carry, or
.Li 0
when the entry was consumed WITHOUT being handed to dispatch.
A zero is
expected for the wakeup pipe and for a poll-remove cancel, and is also the
signature of a completion dropped because its registration was already torn
down -- so a zero alone is not a fault; the join is what matters.
.Pp
Together the four events localize a lost wakeup to one step, and the steps have
different fixes:
.Bl -bullet -offset indent -compact
.It
a
.Dv PARK_TASK
whose task has no
.Dv SUBMIT :
no SQE was ever queued for it.
.It
a
.Dv PARK_TASK
whose task has a
.Dv SUBMIT
but no
.Dv REAP :
the completion never came back from the ring.
.It
a
.Dv REAP
with no
.Dv WAKE :
the reaper consumed it and never handed it to dispatch.
.It
a
.Dv WAKE
with no
.Dv RUN :
dispatch ran; the loss is downstream of it.
.El
.Pp
.Dv XTC_TAIL_SUBMIT
records that an SQE was handed to the kernel, keyed by the task the request is
on behalf of, and
.Dv XTC_TAIL_SUBMIT_FAIL
records that a submission did NOT reach it.
Its
.Fa detail
carries the negated errno when the submit was refused, or -- for a SHORT
submit, which has no errno -- the number of SQEs left behind, offset by
.Dv XTC_TAIL_SHORT_SUBMIT_BASE .
Both shapes matter: the submit call returns a NEGATIVE errno or the NUMBER of
entries it consumed, which can be fewer than were queued, so a short submit
looks like success to an errno-only check while an SQE never reached the
kernel.
Together they split the first bucket above: a completion that never came back
may never have been ASKED for.
.Dv XTC_TAIL_SUBMIT_FAIL
is the only kind here whose mere presence is a fault -- every other needs a
join to mean anything -- because a fiber parked on a refused request can never
be woken.
.Pp
.Nm xtc-tail.py Fl -strands
performs this classification.
Every bucket is an ABSENCE claim, so check
.Fn xtc_tail_dropped
first: in a wrapped ring an absence may only mean the event was evicted.
.Dv XTC_TAIL_REAP
is the highest-volume event here, one per completion, so prefer a short
capture window.
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Bd -literal -offset indent
xtc_tail_enable(XTC_TAIL_SCHED);
/* run the workload */
int fd = open("trace.xtcl", O_WRONLY|O_CREAT|O_TRUNC, 0644);
xtc_tail_dump(fd);          /* versioned binary, for the offline viewer */
close(fd);
xtc_tail_disable();
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr xtc_stats 3 ,
.Xr xtc_trace 3 ,
.Xr xtc_inspect 3 ,
.Xr xtc 7
